<h2><a id="Presentació" name="Presentació"></a>Presentation and options</h2>

<p>This application performs reclassification, mathematical operations, and logical operations on any number of layers of any type (raster, structured vector and non-structured vector layers), regardless of the directories in which they are stored. These operations are based on a mathematical or logical expression involving input layers, constant values, operators, and functions. The result is a raster layer containing the values obtained by evaluating the mathematical or logical expression for each pixel within the geographic extent of the working area.</p>

<p>The application provides the following options for documenting the mathematical or logical expression and the calculation parameters:</p>

 <!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->

<p>For more information about the structure and contents of MMC files, see <a href="#mmc">Description of the parameter file (*.MMC) and mathematical and logical syntax</a>. The section <a href="#mmc-comanda">Correspondence between an MMC file and its command-line syntax</a> provides an example of how the same operation can be expressed using either execution method.</p>

<h3>Input data requirements</h3>

<p>The mathematical expression must contain at least one file, but it does not necessarily need to include any operators or functions. This occurs when the application is used to perform transformations on a layer, such as clipping or scaling its values, modifying the NoData value, changing the data type or format of a raster, or rasterizing a vector layer. In these cases, the mathematical or logical expression must contain at least one input layer, but no operators or functions are required. However, MiraMon provides specific applications for performing these transformations, such as <a href="IMGIMG.htm">IMGIMG</a> and <a href="TiraVec.htm">TiraVec</a>, without using CalcImg.</p>

<p>All rasters included in the expression:</p>

<ul>
    <li>must cover the <b>same geographic extent</b></li>
    <li>must have the <b>same spatial resolution</b></li>
    <li>must use the <b>same units</b> and the <b>same reference system</b></li>
</ul>

<p>However, they may have different data types (byte, real, etc), regardless of their compression level.</p>

<p>For vector input layers, their processing extent is defined by the extent of the raster layers included in the expression, if any. For expressions involving only vector layers, the geographic extent must be specified using the <b>Advanced</b> options button.</p>

<p>Undefined operations or functions (e.g., division by zero, the square root of a negative number, etc) always return the <b>NoData</b> value at the corresponding geographic location. The application determines this value according to the data type of the output file (255 for byte, 0 for bit, and the most negative possible value for all other data types), unless it is explicitly specified in the advanced options dialog box. In the latter case, make sure to select a NoData value outside the range of possible values in the output raster. When in doubt, it is recommended to let the application determine this value.</p>

<p>All calculations are performed using <b>double precision</b> (15-16 significant digits and an approximate magnitude range from 1.7E-308 to 1.7E+308). The <b>length</b> or complexity of the expression is <b>unlimited</b> (subject only to the limitations of the hardware and operating system).</p>

