<p>The conditional function ?(condicio, funcio_TRUE, funcio_FALSE) has not been implemented because it can be reproduced using a combination of logical operations as follows:</p>
<pre>
(condicio)*(funcio_TRUE)+(NOT condicio)*(funcio_FALSE)
</pre>

<h3>Order of precedence</h3>

<p>The order of precedence determines the sequence in which operators and functions in an expression are evaluated. Operators and functions are grouped into six categories, listed from highest to lowest precedence. Those with higher precedence are evaluated before those with lower precedence. Within the same category, operations are evaluated from left to right.</p>

<ul>
    <li>1: ()</li>
    <li>2: + , - [as unary plus and minus], single-argument functions [e.g., SIN, LOG...]</li>
    <li>3: Two-argument functions [e.g., POW, CVR, MAX...]</li>
    <li>4: * , /</li>
    <li>5: + , - [as addition and subtraction operators]</li>
    <li>6: Logical operators [e.g., AND, &gt;=...]</li>
</ul>

<a id="mmc" name="mmc"></a><h3>Description of the parameter file (*.MMC) and mathematical and logical syntax</h3>

<p>The MMC file (.mmc) is a text file that stores the parameters of a mathematical or logical operation performed using the MiraMon layer and field calculator/reclassifier. It contains, among other elements, the following:</p>

<ul>
    <li><b>[Formula]:</b> This section contains information about the mathematical formula to be evaluated.<br/>
    <ul>
        <b>Expressio:</b> A character string representing the mathematical or logical operation to be performed. It consists of numbers, layers, operators, and functions. The layer names included in the mathematical expression are aliases for the corresponding files and are enclosed in square brackets ('[' and ']'). The supported operators and functions are described above. Function arguments must be enclosed in parentheses (e.g., SING(30)). If a function has more than one argument, the arguments must be separated by commas (e.g., MAX([IMG1],[IMG2])). When in doubt, it is recommended to use the syntax generated by clicking the corresponding buttons and selecting the menu options in the Windows application, without subsequently editing it or adding only the necessary parameters.
    </ul>
    <li><b>[Variables]:</b> This section associates the layer aliases used in the formula with the actual file names. The paths to these files are relative to the parameter file.</li>
    <li><b>[Result]:</b> This section contains information about the characteristics of the output file generated by the mathematical operation.<br/>
    <ul>
        <b>Fitxer:</b> Output file name. Its path is relative to the parameter file.<br/>
        <b>Tipus:</b> Output file data type (automatic, byte, byte-RLE, etc). <u>Automatic</u> indicates that the application automatically determines the output data type (integer or real) based on the range and type of the output values, as well as the compression level. In automatic mode, the output is not compressed if at least one of the images included in the expression is uncompressed, except when the output contains only 0 and 1 values, in which case byte-RLE is used. The bit format is generated only when explicitly specified, since the byte-RLE format typically produces smaller files.<br/>
        <b>Titol:</b> Descriptive title of the output file. This field may be left blank if the mathematical or logical formula is to be used as the title.<br/>
        <b>Desc_valors:</b> Character string describing the type of values that will be obtained.<br/>
        <b>Fitxer_categories:</b> For categorical results, the IMG file from which the categories are imported. Its path is relative to the parameter file.<br/>
        <b>Valor_Fons:</b> Background (NoData) value of the output file. The literal NODATA is the default value and indicates that the application will determine the appropriate NoData value.<br/>
        <b>Stretch:</b> A value of 0 indicates that no scaling is applied and that values outside the range of the selected data type are clipped. A value of 1 indicates that the resulting values are linearly rescaled to the interval defined by the minimum and maximum scaling values. The default value is 0, corresponding to clipping.<br/>
        <b>Minim_Stretch:</b> Enabled when Stretch=1; x is the default value for the selected data type.<br/>
        <b>Maxim_Stretch:</b> Enabled when Stretch=1; x is the default value for the selected data type.<br/>
        <b>Truncar:</b> If a real data type is not selected, the results are rounded when Truncar=0 or truncated when Truncar=1. The default value is 0.<br/>
        <b>Reservar_V_Fons:</b> A value of 0 indicates that no value is initially reserved for NoData, whereas a value of 1 indicates that a value is reserved for possible NoData values in the output file. The default value is 0.<br/>
        <b>X_minima, X_maxima, Y_minima, Y_maxima:</b> Define the bounding box of the output raster when no raster layers are included in the mathematical expression.<br/>
        <b>Costat_pixel:</b> Pixel size of the output raster when no raster layers are included in the mathematical expression.<br/></li>
    </ul>
    <li><b>[Reclassificadors]:</b> This section associates the aliases of the reclassification files used in the formula with their actual file names. The paths to these files are relative to the parameter file.</li>
    <li><b>[Alias_vector]:</b> Each vector layer in the variables section has its own section, named after its alias, containing the following elements:<br/>
    <ul>
        <b>base:</b> For structured vector layers, -1 refers to the main table. Otherwise, it specifies the index of the field that establishes the relationship with the related table.<br/>
        <b>camp:</b> For structured vector layers, the index of the field on which calculations are performed (zero-based indexing).<br/>
        <b>repeticio:</b> For structured vector layers with multiple records, the index specifying which record is used: 1, 2... starting from the first record; -1, -2... starting from the last record.<br/>
        <b>valor_exterior:</b> Value assigned to the emulated raster outside the vector features but within the specified geographic extent.<br/>
        <b>CONSIDER_NODATA:</b> For vector layers, specifies that the NoData value is taken into account.<br/>
    </ul>
    </li>
</ul>

<a id="mmc-comanda" name="mmc-comanda"></a><h3>Example of the correspondence between an MMC file and its command-line syntax:</h3>

<p>The following MMC file and command line perform the same calculation. <code>Expressio</code> and <code>Fitxer</code> are the two required arguments, in that order, and are not specified as <code>/Formula_Expressio=</code> or <code>/Sortida_Fitxer=</code>. Any other key is converted into a command-line modifier using the <code>/Seccio_Clau=valor</code> syntax. The <code>[MUNI]</code> section is named after the vector layer alias, so its modifiers begin with <code>MUNI_</code>. Keys not included in this example follow the same mapping.</p>

<p>In an MMC file, a relative path is resolved relative to the directory containing the file. On the command line, it is resolved relative to the current working directory.</p>

<p><b>MMC file:</b></p>
<blockquote>
<pre>
[Formula]
Expressio=RECL([RECL1],[NDVI])*([MUNI]&gt;100)

[Sortida]
Fitxer=resultat.img
Tipus=byte
Titol=NDVI municipal
Valor_Fons=NODATA
TractamentVariable=3

[Variables]
NDVI=ndvi.img
MUNI=municipis.pnt

[Reclassificadors]
RECL1=grups.mrc

[MUNI]
base=-1
camp=3
repeticio=1
CONSIDER_NODATA=0
valor_exterior=NODATA
</pre>
</blockquote>

<p><b>Command line:</b></p>
<blockquote>
<pre>
CalcImg "RECL([RECL1],[NDVI])*([MUNI]&gt;100)" resultat.img /Sortida_Tipus=byte /Sortida_Titol="NDVI municipal" /Sortida_Valor_Fons=NODATA /Sortida_TractamentVariable=3 /Variables_NDVI=ndvi.img /Variables_MUNI=municipis.pnt /Reclassificadors_RECL1=grups.mrc /MUNI_base=-1 /MUNI_camp=3 /MUNI_repeticio=1 /MUNI_CONSIDER_NODATA=0 /MUNI_valor_exterior=NODATA
</pre>
</blockquote>

<h3>Lineage</h3>

<p>When a layer has been reclassified, all the parameters applied during reclassification are recorded in its lineage metadata.</p>

<div align="center"><img src="CalcImg_llinatge.png">   <img src="CalcImg_llinatge2.png"></div>

<p>For more information about CalcImg, see the following reference:</p>

<ul>
    <p><b>Pesquer L, Masó J, Pons X </b>(2000) <i>Herramientas de análisis combinado ráster/vector en un entorno SIG</i>. Aguado I, Gómez M (eds.) Tecnologías Geográficas para el Desarrollo Sostenible. Departamento de Geografía. Universidad de Alcalá, 2000: 53-73. IX congreso del Grupo de Métodos Cuantitativos, Teledetección y SIG de la Asociación de Geógrafos Españoles, Alcalá de Henares.</p>
</ul>

<p><a href="#MENU"><img src="back.jpg"/></a></p>
<hr/>

<h2><a id="Caixa" name="Caixa"></a>Dialog box of the application</h2>

<div align="center">
<table>
<tr>
<td><div align="center"><img src="CalcImg_finestra.png">   <img src="CalcImg_finestra2.png">   <img src="CalcImg_finestra2.png"></div></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td align="center">CalcImg dialog box</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>

<p>Enter the mathematical or logical formula to be evaluated in the first field of the dialog box. The formula consists of numbers, layers (rasters and vectors), operators, and functions. The layer names included in the mathematical expression are aliases for the corresponding files and are enclosed in square brackets ('[' and ']'). These brackets are automatically inserted when a layer is added using the <img src="CalcImg_obrir.png"> buttons.</p>

<p>The layer name displayed in the field corresponds to the file name, without its path, to improve the readability of the expression. The application retains the file path selected in the file browsing dialog box. If files with identical names are located in different directories, the application adds a distinguishing character to ensure that the expression can be evaluated correctly.</p>

<p>When a vector layer is added to the expression, some additional parameters must be specified:</p>

<ul>
    <li><b>External value for the vector:</b> Value that a raster emulating the vector layer would have in areas outside the vector features or in features that do not have a valid attribute. For polygon files, this corresponds to the value of the universal polygon and the value assigned to features with an empty attribute field or a missing corresponding record. For line or point files, this corresponds to the background value of the raster grid on which the lines or points are located, as well as the value assigned to features with an empty attribute field or a missing corresponding record.</li>
    <li><b>Field:</b> For topologically-structured vector layers, specify the field in the main or related database table on which the calculations will be performed. If the field is alphanumeric, it is treated as numeric unless a logical equality or inequality operation involving a constant string is specified. Example: [Inv_for;Municipi;]=&quot;Olot&quot;.</li>
    <li><b>Multiple-record position:</b> For databases with multiple records associated with the same graphic identifier, specifies which record is used in the calculations.<br/>
    After a structured vector layer is selected, the application automatically inserts the field descriptor (or the field name if no descriptor is available) as a comment alongside the alias. <b>Comments</b> are enclosed between two <b>';'</b> characters and may be inserted anywhere in the formula, provided they are properly opened and closed.</li>
</ul>

<p>The <img src="CalcImg_llistat.png"> button displays information about the properties of the layers included in the expression, as well as the correspondence between their aliases and actual file names.</p>

<p>The supported <b>operators</b> and <b>functions</b> are described in the <a href="#operadors">Supported operators and functions</a> section. Function arguments must be enclosed in parentheses, which are automatically inserted when a function is added using the <img src="CalcImg_llibreria.png"> button (e.g., SING(30)). If a function has more than one argument, the arguments must be separated by commas (e.g., MAX([IMG1],[IMG2])).</p>

<p>All operators can be used with both layers and numeric values.<br/>
For example, the following expression can be used:</p>

<pre>
 MAX([IMG1], SING(30))
</pre>

<p>For each cell in the IMG1 layer, the result is the maximum of the cell value and the sine of 30°. Other expressions can also be used, such as:</p>

<pre>
 MAX([IMG1], 100)
 100*SING([IMG1])
 MAX( MIN([IMG1],[IMG2]), 100)
 etc.
</pre>

<p>When in doubt, it is recommended to use the syntax generated by clicking the corresponding buttons and selecting the menu options in the Windows application, without subsequently editing it. It is also recommended to use parentheses whenever there is uncertainty about operator precedence.</p>

<p>The application allows to <b>save the formula</b> to an MMC file. When a BAT file is generated or the OK button is clicked, the dialog box constructs the command line with all the parameters. If the formula has already been saved, the application does not prompt the user to save it again until the expression or one of its parameters is modified. The prompt is displayed only once. When the application is launched from MiraDades, this prompt is not displayed.</p>

<p>The <b>data type</b> of the output file is selected from the corresponding drop-down list. In &quot;Automatic&quot; mode, the application automatically determines the output data type based on the range and type (integer or real) of the output values, as well as the compression level, applying compression whenever appropriate. For example, when the result is Boolean (0, 1), the byte-RLE format is selected, resulting in very small files. &quot;Automatic&quot; mode does not apply compression if at least one of the images included in the expression is uncompressed, except when the results are Boolean (0 or 1), in which case byte-RLE is used. The bit format is generated only when explicitly specified, since the byte-RLE format typically produces smaller files. Although automatic mode is more convenient, calculations are performed considerably faster when the output data type is explicitly specified. In addition, &quot;Automatic&quot; mode requires a temporary file in <i>double</i> format to be created during processing, which may require considerable disk space. The information button associated with the data type displays the amount of disk space required to generate the result. For compressed files, when automatic mode is selected or when the Scale option is enabled, the application displays the maximum disk space that would be required if the files could not be compressed at all. Note that both automatic mode and the Scale option require an additional temporary file.</p>

<p>When <b>&quot;Clip&quot;</b> is selected, if the range of output values exceeds the range of the selected domain (data type), values outside that range are set to the maximum or minimum value of the domain, as appropriate. Alternatively, when <b>scaling</b> is selected, the application linearly rescales the resulting values to the interval specified in the scaling <b>&quot;Minimum&quot;</b> and <b>&quot;Maximum&quot;</b> fields. For example, if a file with values ranging from 0 to 200 is multiplied by 2 and byte is selected as the output data type, selecting Clip assigns a value of 255 to all values between 256 and 400. Selecting Scale instead rescales the range [0,400] to the range [0,255].</p>

<p>When the output data type is not a real-number data type, the application allows you to choose between <b>truncating</b> or <b>rounding</b> (recommended) the result. This operation is applied only during the final stage of writing the output file, not during the calculations. To truncate values at an intermediate stage of the expression, use the ENT() function (integer part), available by clicking the &quot;Function Library&quot; button.</p>

<p>The <b>Title</b> field allows you to enter a descriptive title for the output file. If left blank, the mathematical or logical formula is used as the title.</p>

<p>The <img src="CalcImg_reclassifica.png"> button allows you to define the reclassification function: RECL([reclassification file], [layer to be reclassified]). This function replaces the original values of the layer with new values according to the mappings specified in the reclassification file (*.MRC). This file consists of lines of numeric values specifying these mappings. A line containing two numbers indicates that the first value will be replaced with the second (line 2 in the following example). A line containing three numbers indicates that any value within the interval defined by the first and second numbers (greater than or equal to the first and strictly less than the second) will be replaced with the third (line 3 in the example). This format supports blank lines and comments when the first character of the line is <b>';'</b> (line 1 in the example). Note that the reclassification file format does not require a second ';' character to close a comment.</p>

<p>The reclassification format also supports special syntax for assigning an individual value (line 5 in the example) or a range of values (line 6 in the example) to the background or NoData value by specifying the literal &quot;NODATA&quot; as the new value. The background value can also be reclassified like any other pixel value (line 4 in the example), assigning it a new value so that it is no longer treated as NoData.</p>

<p>The &quot;layer to be reclassified&quot; argument should be interpreted in its broadest sense. It may correspond to one of the selected files or to the result of any operation involving layers and scalar values. Similarly, the reclassification result may be saved as the final output or used as an intermediate result within the mathematical or logical expression.</p>

<p>Examples of valid reclassification formats:</p>

<pre>
;This line is a comment
10 20
1 5 8
NODATA 1
1 NODATA
2 3 NODATA
</pre>

<p>Examples of invalid reclassification formats:</p>

<pre>
This line is an incorrectly defined comment
10
1 5 8 9
NODATA 1 2
1 NODATA 2
NODATA NODATA
</pre>

<p>Example of an expression that reclassifies an intermediate result:</p>

<pre>
RECL([nivells_altimetrics], [MDE]*10)
</pre>

<p>In this example, a Digital Elevation Model (DEM) expressed in decimeters is converted to meters and then reclassified into elevation zones using the &quot;nivells_altimetrics.MRC&quot; file.</p>

<p>When the <img src="CalcImg_avancades.png"> button is clicked, the <b>&quot;Value description&quot;</b> field allows you to enter a descriptive string specifying the type of values that will be obtained. This descriptor is displayed in parentheses when a query by location is performed on the output file. Generally, this field should be left blank for categorical results (e.g., thematic maps), while the appropriate units should be specified for maps containing continuous values (e.g., &quot;°C&quot; for a map generated using a regression model that estimates temperature in degrees Celsius). For categorical results, the output file can import the categories associated with any layer (described in the DOC file or the associated DBF table for raster images), particularly from one of the images included in the mathematical or logical expression. If a category file is required, it must be specified in the <b>&quot;Category file&quot;</b> field. This makes it easy to automatically document the categories of a categorical output file when a reference IMG file is available.</p>

<p>The advanced options also allow you to specify the NoData value of the output file. It is recommended to always use automatic mode, except in special cases where a specific NoData value must be assigned.</p>

<p>The <b>&quot;Reserve the NoData value from the start&quot;</b> advanced option is useful when it is known in advance that the output file will contain NoData values. Even if this option is not enabled, should the application detect that a NoData value needs to be written to the output file, it will make the necessary adjustments to accommodate it.</p>

<p>When no raster layers are included in the formula, the geographic extent of the output raster must be defined by specifying its bounding box coordinates and pixel size in the corresponding fields.</p>

<p>Like all MiraMon Support Applications launched from their corresponding Windows interfaces, the calculator saves the parameters of each execution to a text file, allowing the parameters of the most recent execution to be retrieved. However, to save a specific expression for later use, regardless of any calculations performed in the meantime, click the <b>&quot;Save...&quot;</b> button. The formula is saved to the specified MMC file, except for the reserved file name (_PARCLMM.MMC), which is used internally by the application to store the most recent execution. To retrieve a previously saved formula, simply click the <b>&quot;Load&quot;</b> button. If a NoData value was explicitly specified during the most recent execution, the <b>'!'</b> symbol next to the &quot;Advanced...&quot; button indicates that it is <u>strongly recommended</u> to restore automatic mode if the new execution does not require the specific background value previously defined.</p>

<p><a href="#MENU"><img src="back.jpg"/></a></p>
<hr/>

<h2><a id="Exemples" name="Exemples"></a>Examples</h2>

<p><b>Example 1:</b> We want to create an image as if it had already been multiplied by a mask: Suppose we want to select from image 'A' all zones which have values bigger than 3000 in image 'B'.<br/> 
The operation will be: <font face="Courier New" size="-1">[A]*([B]&gt;3000)</font><br/> 
In this case, the resulting image will have the value 0 in all zones with values that are equal or less than 3000 in image 'B', and will maintain the original values in 'A' in the rest of the image (zones with values greater than 3000).

<p><br/>
<b>Example 2:</b> We want to reclassify the values of an image 'C' in two groups. All values that are less than 100 will become 50 and values equal or greater than 100 will become 150.<br/> 
The operation will be: <font face="Courier New" size="-1">([C]&lt;100)*50+(NOT([C]&lt;100))*150.</font><br/> 
Alternatively, the following form can be used: <font face="Courier New" size="-1">([C]&lt;100)*50+([C]&gt;=100)*150.</font><br/> 
The resulting image will have two different values: 50 for those that where smaller than 100 and 150 for those that where greater than 100.<br/> 
The function of reclassification can also be used: <font face="Courier New" size="-1">RECL([MMReclass],[C])</font> if we do know the range of values in the raster 'C'. If 'C' is a raster of type byte or byte-RLE the contents of the reclassification file MMReclass.MRC would be:

<blockquote>
<pre>
0 100 50
100 256 150 
</pre>
</blockquote>or, if we do not know the range of values for raster 'C', a reclassification based in a MRC file such as the following is also admitted:<br/>

<blockquote>
<pre>
-1.0E+40 100 50
100 1.0E+40 150
</pre>
</blockquote>

<p><br/>
<b>Example 3:</b> Suppose we want to obtain a raster file with the following grouped categories:</p>

<blockquote>
<pre>
category1 : Dense forest 
category2 : Sparse forest 
category3 : Riparian forests
category4 : Reforestations
category5 : Poplar plantations
category6 : Platanus plantations
category7 : Shrublands 
category8 : Wetlands
category9 : Meadows
category 10 : CORINE Fires
category 11 : Rock outcrops
category 12 : Scree
category 13 : Bare soil
category 14 : Beaches
category 15 : Continental waters 
category 16 : Glaciers and permanent snow
category 17 : Cultivation fields
category 18 : Urban zones
category 19 : Roads 
category 20 : Sport zones
category 21 : Quarrying zones
</pre>
</blockquote>according to the reclassification:

<blockquote>
<pre>
1 11 1
11 17 2
17 3
18 21 4
</pre>
</blockquote>The resulting raster will have four categories which could be defined as:

<blockquote>
<pre>
category1 : Forests
category2 : Non-productive natural zones
category3 : Cultivation fields
category4 : Non-productive artificial zones
</pre>
</blockquote>

<p><br/>
<b>Example 4:</b> Given an image, 256c, if you want to assign value 1 to all values that are equal to &quot;3&quot; or to &quot;12&quot; and &quot;0&quot; to the rest of the values, then you will write:</p>

<blockquote>
<pre>
([256c]=3)OR([256c]=12)
</pre>
</blockquote>This operation can also be done from within MiraMon through a query by attributes and saving the file as a IMG file.

<p><br/>
<b>Example 5:</b> Given an image, 256c, in order to select both values 3 and 12, but preserving these values in the result and making the rest of the values as 0, we will write:<br/></p>

<blockquote>
<pre>
(([256c]=3)OR([256c]=12))*[256c]
</pre>
</blockquote>

<p><br/>
<b>Example 6:</b> How to obtain the digital elevation model from three States out of the digital elevation model of a of USA '[USA_DEM]' and the vector layer of the counties '[States]'.</p>

<blockquote>
<pre>
[USA_DEM]*([States;State;]=&quot;Connecticut&quot; OR [States;State;]=&quot;Wisconsin&quot; OR [States;State;]=&quot;Idaho&quot;)
</pre>
</blockquote>It should be noted that the resulting file will have the same extent as [USA_DEM], the whole USA, and the <a href="Retalla.htm">Retalla</a> application needs to be used in order to reduce the extent.

<p><br/>
<b>Example 7:</b> The rasterization of an arc layer, '[Profiles]', converting rasterized lines to 0 value and the external zone to 1 is a less intuitive but interesting example that shows how dynamic rasterization is treated inside the calculator.</p>

<blockquote>
<pre>
[Profiles;LONG_ARC;]=0
where we have defined 'Value external to the vector'=0
</pre>
</blockquote>There is no arc with longitude equal to 0 in the cells affected by the vector (rasterized) and, thus, the logical operation returns FALSE, that is 0. On the other hand, as the external zone has been assigned the value 0, which compared to value 0, the result is TRUE, that is 1.

 <p><br/> 
 <b>Example 8:</b> We need to define a random value between -1 and 1. Raster1 is a raster file with the constant value 1, is necessary to define the extent and the pixel size of the output file.

<blockquote>
<pre>
(RND(2)-1)*[Raster1]
	</pre>
	 </blockquote>
<p><a href="#MENU"><img src="back.jpg"/></a></p>
  <hr/>

<exclusiva>
<h2><a id="Sintaxi" name="Sintaxi"></a>Syntax</h2>
<!-- Aquí inicia la part corresponent a la sintaxi de miramon. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpssintaxi"/>
<p>Command line: <code>CalcImg &lt;Expression&gt; &lt;OutputFile&gt; [/NomSeccio_NomClau=value ...]</code>. The expression and the output file are the only two mandatory arguments, in that order. Every other parameter is a modifier. Some must appear for the formula to make sense: if the expression uses an alias, its file has to be given, for example <code>/Variables_NDVI=ndvi.img</code> when there is <code>[NDVI]</code>. The others may be omitted. If <code>/Sortida_Tipus=</code> is omitted the type is <code>auto</code>, or <code>double-string</code> when <code>/Sortida_CampResultat=</code> is present. A single <code>.mmc</code> file is still accepted. <code>/Formula_Expressio=</code> and <code>/Sortida_Fitxer=</code> are not valid modifiers.</p>
<p>A command-line parameter cannot contain the character <code>&quot;</code>, because these quotes delimit the argument. When the value contains one, a dieresis <code>¨</code> is written and CalcImg converts it back to <code>&quot;</code>. The quotes that surround a whole argument are not part of the value: they only group the text when there are spaces or special characters, as in <code>&quot;RECL([RECL1],[NDVI])*([MUNI]&gt;100)&quot;</code> or <code>/Sortida_Titol=&quot;NDVI municipal&quot;</code>. If the title is to be <code>NDVI &quot;municipal&quot;</code>, the line is written <code>/Sortida_Titol=&quot;NDVI ¨municipal¨&quot;</code> and CalcImg reads <code>NDVI &quot;municipal&quot;</code>. The same applies to the expression, a file name or any other parameter. The window already does this when generating the BAT or when running with OK. In an MMC file, quotes are stored as they are.</p>
<!-- Aquí acaba la part corresponent a la sintaxi de miramon editable de el WPS.-->

</exclusiva>
